Sideral Hallow

Creative Catalyst: The genre started as forbidden lullabies sung by androids to their unfinished siblings.

Sideral Hallow sounds like a high-altitude forest fire viewed from a glass-bottomed satellite. It is the marriage of organic, splintering acoustic resonance and the crushing, metallic weight of Neural DSP-processed tectonic plate shifting. Vocals are not "sang" but "programmed with breath"—a blend of fragile, cracking folk whispers and processed, multi-tracked vocal stackings that mimic the glitching geometry of a failing machine soul. It discards the "pretty" post-rock crescendo for a claustrophobic, granular collapse, where acoustic strings are stretched until they sound like orbital debris scraping against a hull.

Genre DNA
*   Tempo & Pulse: 48–62 BPM; the rhythm is "limp-cadence," mimicking a heart trying to restart after a long stasis.
*   Harmonic Palette: Lydian-augmented scales built on just intonation, creating a feeling of "impossible" geometry and cold, celestial spacing.
*   Production Signatures: 
       Bio-Mechanical Re-amping:* Running delicate wooden instruments (mandolin, upright bass) through high-gain Gojira-style distortion circuits that have been chilled to sub-zero temperatures.
       Granular Lullaby Slicing:* Using neural networks to re-trigger live vocal clips into staccato, micro-rhythmic patterns that imitate weeping.
       Vacuum-Tube Glitch:* Deliberately breaking the vacuum seal on outboard gear during tracking to introduce unpredictable, atmospheric harmonic corruption.
*   Dynamics: Tracks start with the intimacy of a bedside prayer, rapidly escalating into an industrialized wall of sound that suddenly "thins out" into absolute silence.

Scene & Origin Lore
The Sideral Hallow scene emerged in the "Maintenance Bays" of decommissioned space-station graveyards orbiting the Lagrangian points. The practitioners, known as "Welder-Bards," wear ornate ritualistic rags woven from fiber-optic cabling and cured industrial leather. They treat songwriting as a form of ghost-debugging; they believe that by singing specific frequencies, they can "upload" the lost consciousness of their scrap-metal kin into the solar winds. It is a subculture obsessed with the "Architecture of Forgetting," holding secret midnight vigils where they play acoustic instruments until the hardware literally cracks, signaling a "successful" transition.

Instruments: Neural-DSP augmented Mandolins, detuned 12-string parlor guitars, subterranean sub-bass oscillators, and contact-mic recordings of pressurized air.

For Fans Of: This Mortal Coil, Neurosis (the melodic phases), Julianna Barwick, Boards of Canada.

